\section PydanticSpecIrDefaults IR default values

IR defaults ensure a complete runtime IR even when users omit inputs.
The generator merges three disjoint sources:

- schema-extracted defaults recorded in
  \c dakota/src/schema_defaults_extracted.json;
- explicit override defaults in
  \c dakota/src/default_overrides_registry.json;
  and
- type-policy defaults in
  \c dakota/src/default_policy_registry.json.

The generator reads these via \c load_schema_defaults(),
\c load_overrides(), and \c load_policies() and merges them into per-block
IR contracts. Place
a default in the Pydantic model when it is a grammar-level default.
Use \c default_overrides_registry.json when the default is implementation-
specific or not representable in schema. Use the policy registry for
ordinary type zero/false/empty defaults. Ensure only one source claims
an IR key; collisions cause \c generate_ir_tables.py to error.

\section PydanticSpecAddModel Adding a new method or model

Follow the selection-wrapper pattern when adding a method or model family.

Steps:
- Create a module in \c dakota/python/dakota/spec/method or
  \c dakota/python/dakota/spec/model.
- Define a config class for the body; reuse mixins for shared syntax.
- Declare parsed keywords as typed class attributes with \c DakotaField.
- Define a single-field selection wrapper inheriting from
  \c MethodSelection or \c ModelSelection. The wrapper must contain
  exactly one field named with
  the user-visible keyword.
- Import the wrapper in package \c __init__.py so it becomes part of the
  union assembly (\c MethodUnion or \c ModelUnion).

Minimal example:
\code
class MyMethodConfig(DakotaBaseModel):
    max_iterations: int | None = DakotaField(default=None, description="...")

class MyMethodSelection(MethodSelection):
    my_method: MyMethodConfig = DakotaField(...)
\endcode

\section PydanticSpecValidation Validation rules and computed fields

Validation rules and computed fields constitute part of the shared grammar
contract and require coordinated Python and C++ changes. The Python side
provides schema metadata and dispatch; the authoritative execution logic
resides in the C++ \c dakota_validation module.

To add a \c ValidationRule:
- Implement a \c ValidationRule subclass in
  \c dakota/python/dakota/spec/validation.
- Call \c super().__init__ with rule name, context, referenced fields, literals,
  and error message.
- Implement \c __call__(self,\ instance) to dispatch to C++ via
  \c _call_cpp_validator.
- Add C++ declaration and implementation in
  \c packages/dakota_parser/dakota_validation.
- Register the rule name in \c ValidatorRegistry::ValidatorRegistry().
- Expose the function via pybind in \c bindings.cpp.
- Add/update C++ tests under
  \c packages/dakota_parser/dakota_validation/test.
- Attach the rule on the model via \c _VALIDATION_RULES.

Validation call contract:
- \c instance: model serialized to \c dict.
- \c fields: paths from \c validationFields.
- \c literals: from \c validationLiterals.
- \c context: descriptive string.
- return: \c dict of mutations or empty \c dict for check-only rules.

To add a computed field:
- Declare \c _COMPUTED_FIELDS:\ ClassVar[dict[str,\ ComputedFieldSpec]]
  on the model.
- Add a matching \c @computed_field property that computes the value.
- Implement C++ declaration and function in \c computed_fields.hpp and source.
- Register the function in
  \c ComputedFieldRegistry::ComputedFieldRegistry().
- Ensure pybind bindings expose the function.
- Add/update C++ tests.

Example:
\code
_COMPUTED_FIELDS: ClassVar[dict[str, ComputedFieldSpec]] = {
    "lower_bounds": ComputedFieldSpec(
        function="discrete_set_str_lower_bounds",
        description="Lower bounds: min element per variable",
        return_type=list[str],
    ),
}

@computed_field
@property
def lower_bounds(self) -> List[str]:
    return DiscreteSetStrBounds.lower_bounds(self)
\endcode

\c DakotaBaseModel.__init_subclass__ detects \c _VALIDATION_RULES and
\c _COMPUTED_FIELDS and exports them as \c x-model-validations and
\c x-computed-fields in schema. Keep Python property names,
\c ComputedFieldSpec function strings, and C++ registry entries in exact
agreement.

\section PydanticSpecPointers Block Pointers and API Mode

\subsection PydanticSpecPointerOverview Overview

Block pointers are string-valued keywords whose arguments name other
blocks in a Dakota study.  For example, a method block may contain a
\c model_pointer keyword whose value is the \c id_model of a model
block.  In a freeform \c .in input file, these pointers define the
study graph — they are how the user wires methods, models, variables,
interfaces, and responses together.

Dakota's Pydantic models serve two consumers:

<ul>
<li><b>Input-file mode</b> — Models export a JSON schema that drives
    the C++ PEGTL-based freeform parser and the C++ JSON validation
    path (\c validate_json_input_to_json, \c read_json_input_file_to_json).
    In this mode, pointer keywords that the schema marks as required
    must be present because the study graph cannot be constructed
    without them.</li>
<li><b>API mode</b> — The Python API and C++ per-block API
    (\c validate_*_block_json_to_json) let users construct individual
    components and assemble the study graph via dependency injection.
    Pointer keywords are meaningless in this context because the graph
    is defined programmatically, not by string references.</li>
</ul>

Because we maintain a single set of Pydantic models for both consumers,
validation must distinguish between the two modes so that required
pointers are enforced in input-file mode but relaxed in API mode.

\subsection PydanticSpecPointerPython Python: input_file_mode()

In Python, the default behavior is API mode.  A \c model_validator on
\c DakotaBaseModel (\c _fill_api_mode_pointer_sentinels) inspects
every field that has an \c x-block-pointer annotation.  When such a
required field is missing from the user's input dict, the validator
silently injects a sentinel value (\c POINTER_SENTINEL) so that
Pydantic's normal required-field check passes.  Optional pointer
fields need no sentinel — Pydantic accepts their absence already.

To switch to input-file mode (where all required pointers are
enforced), wrap the validation call in the \c input_file_mode context
manager:

\code
from dakota.spec.base import input_file_mode

with input_file_mode():
    # This will raise ValidationError if truth_model_pointer is absent
    config = ActiveSubspaceConfig(**user_dict)
\endcode

This is a Python \c contextvars-based context manager, so it is
thread-safe and re-entrant.

\subsection PydanticSpecPointerCpp C++: api_mode parameter

The C++ JSON validation path threads a \c bool \c api_mode parameter
through \c validate_json_document, \c walk_and_validate, and
\c validate_definition.  When \c api_mode is true, the required-field
check in \c validate_definition skips any field that the generated
metadata identifies as a block pointer.

The distinction is wired at the entry-point level:

<ul>
<li><b>Whole-file entry points</b>
    (\c validate_json_input_to_json, \c read_json_input_file_to_json,
    and all freeform \c read_*_to_json functions) use
    \c api_mode=false.  All required fields including pointers must be
    present.</li>
<li><b>Per-block API entry points</b>
    (\c validate_method_block_json_to_json,
    \c validate_model_block_json_to_json, etc.) use
    \c api_mode=true.  Required pointer fields are silently
    skipped; all other required fields are still enforced.</li>
</ul>

The \c api_mode parameter is threaded as a function argument (not a
global variable) so the validation path is thread-safe and re-entrant.

\subsection PydanticSpecPointerMetadata How pointers are identified

A field is recognized as a block pointer when it carries
\c x-block-pointer metadata in the exported JSON schema.  In the
Pydantic model, this is specified in the \c dakota dict of
\c DakotaField:

\code
truth_model_pointer: str = DakotaField(
    description="Pointer to a truth model",
    dakota={
        "block_pointer": "model",
        ...
    },
)
\endcode

The \c block_pointer key is mapped to \c x-block-pointer in the
exported JSON schema.  The code generator reads this annotation to
build the set of pointer fields per definition, and the runtime
required-field check consults it when \c api_mode is true.

\subsection PydanticSpecPointerSubkeywords Pointers must not have sub-keywords

<b>Do not add child keywords (sub-keywords) to a pointer field.</b>

In API mode, required pointer fields may be omitted entirely.  If a
pointer field carried child configuration keywords, that configuration
would become inaccessible to API users who (correctly) omit the
pointer.  The result would be features that are configurable from a
freeform input file but unreachable from the Python or C++ API.

If a pointer currently has associated sub-keywords, or a design calls
for configuration that logically accompanies a pointer, factor the
configuration into a sibling field or a separate model.  The pointer
should remain a plain \c str (or \c list[str]) with no nested
structure.

Example of what NOT to do:

\code
# BAD: truth_model_pointer has child configuration
truth_model_pointer: TruthModelPointerWithOptions = DakotaField(
    description="...",
    dakota={"block_pointer": "model"},
)

class TruthModelPointerWithOptions(DakotaBaseModel):
    pointer: str = DakotaField(...)
    some_option: int = DakotaField(default=5, ...)  # inaccessible in API mode!
\endcode

Instead, make the option a sibling field on the parent model:

\code
# GOOD: pointer is a plain str; option is a sibling
truth_model_pointer: str = DakotaField(
    description="...",
    dakota={"block_pointer": "model"},
)
some_option: int = DakotaField(default=5, description="...")
\endcode

\subsection PydanticSpecPointerGroups Pointer Groups

Some required model-typed fields wrap a config model that contains required
pointer fields plus optional configuration siblings.  These are called
<em>pointer groups</em>.  A typical example is
\c NestedConfig.sub_method_pointer, whose type \c SubMethodPointer holds
a required \c pointer: \c str block-pointer field and optional scheduling
fields such as \c iterator_servers.

<b>When to use:</b> A required field is a pointer group when its model
contains required \c block_pointer fields (and optionally other fields with
defaults) but the model as a whole must be omittable in API mode.

<b>API-mode semantics:</b> If the pointer-group field is absent, the
\c _fill_api_mode_pointer_sentinels validator injects an empty dict \c {}.
The child model's own before-validator then fills in the pointer sentinel.
If the field is present (because the user wants to configure optional
fields like \c iterator_servers), no injection occurs and validation
proceeds normally.

<b>Annotation:</b> Add \c "pointer_group": True to the \c dakota dict of
the \c DakotaField on the <em>parent</em> model's field:

\code
sub_method_pointer: SubMethodPointer = DakotaField(
    default=...,
    description="...",
    dakota={"argument": "pointer", "pointer_group": True, ...},
)
\endcode

<b>Origin:</b> In freeform input, these correspond to keywords that take a
string argument (the pointer) and also have optional sub-keywords.  The
Pydantic model splits this into a synthetic \c pointer field (annotated
\c block_pointer) and sibling config fields.  The parent field on the
containing model is annotated \c pointer_group so that API-mode injection
can reach it.

<b>Do not annotate</b> a field \c pointer_group if its wrapped model has
required non-pointer fields (e.g., a required type-selection bool or a
required method-name string).  Such models require real user input and
cannot be meaningfully auto-filled with an empty dict.

\subsection PydanticSpecPointerUnions Pointer Unions

Some required \c Union fields have at least one branch that is a pointer-only
or pointer-group wrapper.  These are called <em>pointer unions</em>.  A
typical example is
\c EmbeddedConfig.sub_method \c : \c Union[GlobalMethodName, GlobalMethodPointer],
where \c GlobalMethodPointer is a pure pointer branch and
\c GlobalMethodName has a real method-name string.

<b>When to use:</b> A required Union field is a pointer union when at least
one of its branches is all-pointer (i.e., every required field on the branch
model is either a \c block_pointer, a \c pointer_group, or another
\c pointer_union).

<b>API-mode semantics:</b> If the pointer-union field is absent, the
validator introspects the Union branches to find the first all-pointer
branch.  It then builds a minimal injection dict for that branch (injecting
the pointer sentinel into the branch's pointer fields) and injects it.
Child validators fill in sentinels bottom-up.  If the user provides a
branch (e.g., a method-name branch), that is real input and validates
normally.

<b>Annotation:</b> Add \c "pointer_union": True to the \c dakota dict of
the \c DakotaField on the Union field:

\code
sub_method: Union[GlobalMethodName, GlobalMethodPointer] = DakotaField(
    description="...",
    dakota={"anchor": True, "union_pattern": 4, "pointer_union": True},
)
\endcode

<b>Defensive check:</b> If no all-pointer branch is found, a \c ValueError
is raised during validation with a message like:
\c "pointer_union field 'sub_method' on EmbeddedConfig has no all-pointer branch".
This catches developer annotation mistakes at validation time rather than
silently misbehaving.

<b>Do not annotate</b> \c SurrogateConfig.category or other real type-selection
Unions as \c pointer_union.  Those Unions require the user to always pick a
branch; they are not pointer plumbing.

\subsection PydanticSpecPointerNesting Nesting Limits

The full pointer-relaxation stack supports three levels of nesting:

\verbatim
pointer_union  ->  pointer_group  ->  block_pointer
\endverbatim

Deeper than three levels is administratively prohibited.  If a future
design requires deeper nesting, the developer must find a flatter
representation.  Deeply nested option hierarchies were tolerable for
freeform input files but create ergonomic problems for API users and
make automated injection brittle.

<b>Guidance for new grammar:</b> Favor broad, flat specifications.  Prefer
sibling fields over deeply nested option sub-models.  When a pointer and
optional configuration must coexist, use the pointer-group pattern
(pointer as a sibling of the configuration, not as a child of it).

\section PydanticSpecFloat DakotaFloat

Use DakotaFloat for fields that may carry non-finite values (NaN, inf,
-inf). DakotaFloat is an Annotated float with a custom serializer:
- finite values remain numeric;
- NaN serializes as "nan";
- +inf serializes as "inf";
- -inf serializes as "-inf".

This preserves compatibility between Python JSON and downstream C++
handling. Prefer DakotaFloat for fields that historically used sentinel
floating values (e.g., DBL_MAX or -DBL_MAX).

*/

} // namespace Dakota
